    <article class="dw-guide">
      <p class="dw-muted"><a href="/diagnostic-writing/">← Teacher dashboard</a> · <a href="/diagnostic-writing/algorithms/">Scoring algorithms</a></p>
      <h1>How to read the Summer Writing Diagnostic</h1>
      <p class="dw-lede">A teacher-facing guide for the dashboard, percentile comparisons, and calibration.</p>

      <h2>What this tool is for</h2>
      <p>
        The diagnostic is a <strong>5-minute timed first draft</strong> of a personal summer narrative.
        It helps you quickly see <em>typing stamina</em>, <em>mechanics patterns</em>, and <em>craft signals</em>
        so you can group students, plan mini-lessons, and decide who needs a follow-up conference.
        It is <strong>not</strong> a final grade or a full writing portfolio assessment.
      </p>

      <h2>Score bands (not letter grades)</h2>
      <p>Three main categories — <strong>Typing</strong>, <strong>Mechanics</strong>, and <strong>Story</strong> — plus <strong>Overall</strong> (the simple average of those three).</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Range</th><th>How to use it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Strong</strong></td><td>80–100</td><td>Stretch goals, peer modeling, enrichment</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>On track</strong></td><td>65–79</td><td>Grade-level first-draft expectations</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Developing</strong></td><td>50–64</td><td>Targeted practice in one skill area</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Needs support</strong></td><td>below 50</td><td>Scaffolds, small group, typing or writing intervention</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Reading peer comparisons (percentiles)</h2>
      <p>
        When you open a student’s detail view, the <strong>comparison panel</strong> shows how that student ranks
        <em>within their class</em> and <em>within their grade</em> — using <strong>percentile ranks</strong>, not raw point differences.
        A 72nd percentile in Typing means this student scored higher than about 72% of classmates on that skill.
      </p>

      <h3>What you see, top to bottom</h3>
      <ol class="dw-steps">
        <li><strong>Summary strip</strong> — Counts how many skills fall at or above the 60th percentile, in the 40th–59th range (near median), or below the 40th percentile.</li>
        <li><strong>Quick grid</strong> — Typing, Mechanics, and Story at a glance: raw score plus <em>nth percentile in class</em> and <em>nth percentile in grade</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Percentile rank in class</strong> — One row per skill. The horizontal scale runs from 0 to 100 (percentile). The green marker is the student; the blue line is the class median (50th percentile); the purple marker (when available) is grade-level standing. The shaded band is the middle 50% of the class (25th–75th percentile). Footer labels show the raw scores at the 25th and 75th percentiles.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional radar</strong> — Collapsed by default. Shows the student’s percentile shape vs. a 50th-percentile reference. Each axis is percentile rank, not raw score.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>How to interpret percentiles</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Percentile</th><th>Plain language</th><th>Teaching move</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>90th+</strong></td><td>Top of class on this skill</td><td>Peer model, extension tasks</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>75th–89th</strong></td><td>Above most classmates</td><td>Stretch within the skill; check for uneven profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>60th–74th</strong></td><td>Above median</td><td>On track; note which skill lags</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>40th–59th</strong></td><td>Near class median</td><td>Typical for the room — pair with absolute score band</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>25th–39th</strong></td><td>Below median</td><td>Small-group or conference on that skill</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Below 25th</strong></td><td>Lower range for the class</td><td>Intervention, scaffolds, read the story yourself</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="dw-callout">
        <strong>Small classes &amp; privacy</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>With fewer than three classmates, percentiles are hidden or marked “small sample” — averages swing easily.</li>
          <li>Use comparisons for <strong>relative placement</strong> in your planning, not for ranking students publicly.</li>
          <li>Percentiles describe standing among peers; they do not replace reading the actual story.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Skill areas at a glance</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Typing</strong> — WPM and how much was written in five minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Mechanics</strong> — Spelling, conventions, and sentence building combined.</li>
        <li><strong>Story</strong> — One combined score for voice, sensory detail, sequence, and word choice.</li>
        <li><strong>Overall</strong> — Average of Typing + Mechanics + Story.</li>
        <li><strong>Minnesota standards tags</strong> — Mapped from the scores above; “needs support” suggests a next step, not a failing grade.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How scores are calibrated</h2>
      <p>
        Rubrics are tuned from <strong>157 live student submissions</strong> (Aug 2026), published middle-school typing/writing research,
        and adult exemplar samples in the <strong>Teacher's Lounge</strong> (Cecelia Everette anchors the top of the scale).
        Grade-level comparison norms use 6th / 7th / 8th ELA and tech classes with a single grade — not mixed-grade Media Arts, Game Design, or Video Production.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Typing</strong> — Calibrated to real 5-minute <em>composition</em> speed. G6 median ≈ 17 WPM / 86 words; strong G6 writers ≈ 29 WPM / 140+ words.</li>
        <li><strong>Story</strong> — Rewards voice, length, and craft; includes fairness floors so genuine narratives are not punished for missing narrow keyword lists.</li>
        <li><strong>Strong band (~80+)</strong> — Should reflect advanced work in your grade, not perfection. Scores near 100 are rare for students and normal for Teacher's Lounge exemplars.</li>
        <li>After rubric updates, run <strong>Re-analyze all</strong> so stored submissions pick up the new calibration.</li>
        <li>Full algorithm reference: <a href="/diagnostic-writing/algorithms/">Scoring algorithms doc</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Read class — scroll through every story</h2>
      <p>
        On the Teacher dashboard, switch from <strong>Scores table</strong> to <strong>Read class</strong>.
        Pick a class (or keep “All classes”), then scroll a feed of every submission with name, score chips, and the full story text.
        Use the student list on the left to jump to someone quickly. Click <strong>Analytics</strong> on any card when you want charts and standards detail.
        <strong>Print this list</strong> opens a clean printable version for annotating offline.
      </p>

      <h2>Teacher’s Lounge</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <strong>Teacher’s Lounge</strong> class (code: <code>alwayslearning</code>) when adults submit sample writing for calibration.
        Those entries are <strong>excluded from student peer averages and percentile pools</strong> so they don’t skew class comparisons.
      </p>

      <div class="dw-callout">
        <strong>Limits of algorithmic analysis</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Cannot judge <em>meaning</em>, humor, cultural context, or trauma-sensitive content — read the story yourself.</li>
          <li>Misses strong ideas hidden behind spelling errors or typing struggles.</li>
          <li>Counts patterns (sensory words, transitions) but not whether they’re used well.</li>
          <li>Does not detect plagiarism, AI-generated text, or paste attempts beyond blocking paste in the UI.</li>
          <li>English learners may score low on mechanics/vocabulary while showing strong thinking — pair scores with your knowledge of the student.</li>
          <li>One timed draft is a snapshot; growth requires multiple samples over time.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Suggested workflow</h2>
      <ol class="dw-steps">
        <li>Filter by class; use <strong>Read class</strong> to read every story in one scroll.</li>
        <li>Scan typing tiers in the scores table for intervention groups.</li>
        <li>Open 2–3 detail views per band to read actual stories.</li>
        <li>Check percentile panels for outliers (one skill far below the 40th percentile).</li>
        <li>Plan one mini-lesson from a common “needs support” standard.</li>
        <li>Re-run <strong>Re-analyze all</strong> after rubric updates — student text is never changed.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="dw-muted" style="margin-top: 40px; font-size: 16px;">Questions? Talk to Mr. Phil. Close this tab to return to the dashboard.</p>
    </article>
